ince 2017 using the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) and a set of modular hardware.  While ISS testing has been completed for most of the studies in the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments (ACME) project, it is expected to continue into 2022.  The objectives and selected findings for each investigation are briefly discussed after a short review of the advantages of studying combustion in microgravity, previous research conducted in space, and the experimental hardware and its operation.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"country":"JP","submissionId":20210021781,"endDate":"2021-10-15T04:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"Japan Society of Microgravity Application"}},"meetingId":"2450efbe0e7f46d7bb61be2036aaea32","id":"3cdb91c30a4045298d6d76125c10cb4a"}],"name":"33rd Japanese Conference on Microgravity Science and Application","location":"Virtual","id":"2450efbe0e7f46d7bb61be2036aaea32","startDate":"2021-10-13T04:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"JASMAC ACME paper 20210921.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20210021781/downloads/JASMAC%20ACME%20paper%2020210921.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20210021781/downloads/JASMAC%20ACME%20paper%2020210921.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20210021781/downloads/JASMAC%20ACME%20paper%2020210921.pdf.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"}